</section>

<section class="block" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head"><h2 id="about-h">How this worked</h2></div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step"><span class="fig" data-k="st-starters">36</span><h3>Starter statements</h3><p data-k="st-starters-p">The conversation opened with 36 statements for people to agree or disagree with.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig" data-k="st-people">77</span><h3>People took part</h3><p data-k="st-people-p">Since 30 October they have voted agree, disagree or pass, and added statements of their own.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig">3</span><h3>Groups emerged</h3><p>Polis, an open-source tool, found three groups of people who voted alike. Nobody chose a group. We named them.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig" data-k="st-voters">39</span><h3>Voters set priorities</h3><p data-k="st-voters-p">On 19 November they spread a budget of credits across 13 possible changes, using quadratic voting.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <details class="fine">
      <summary>Notes for checking the numbers</summary>
      <div class="arows">
        <div class="arow"><h3>Reading the percentages</h3><p data-k="n-pct">Each figure is the share of a group who agreed, out of those in the group who voted on that statement. Later statements had fewer voters, so where fewer than 20 people in a group voted, we show how many. Group 3 has 19 people, so every figure for it shows how many voted, and Group 1 has 20, so most of its figures do too. We apply the 7-in-10 bar before rounding, so a statement shown at 70% can still fall just short.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Reading the map</h3><p>We rebuilt the map from the published votes using the same method as Polis, so people sit close to where the Polis report places them, but not in exactly the same spots. Left to right is the main divide in the votes. <span data-k="n-overlap">The groups overlap on it, Groups 1 and 2 most of all. What separates those two is mainly a second question, whether to set migration numbers by housing and services, which a single line can’t show.</span> Where many people sit close together, their dots pile up, so a dot can sit a little to one side of its exact place.</p><p>Each person’s card shows up to three of their votes as examples. For each group they sided with, it shows the statement most of that group agreed with (or rejected), judged cautiously when few people voted, preferring statements the person agreed with over ones they rejected. A statement that fits two groups is shown once.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>How we found three groups</h3><p data-k="n-three">Polis, the tool that grouped people, found three groups here. It tries two, three, four and five groups (k-means clustering) and keeps the number that separates people most clearly (a silhouette score). Here three only just beat two, so as more people join it could settle on two. We show the groups Polis finds. We add one of our own only where a group splits clearly in two, and that takes more than four statements.</p><p data-k="n-distinct">The groups differ clearly. On each of these statements, at least 65% of one group agreed and at most 35% of the other, with at least 10 people voting in each, and a gap that big would happen by chance less than once in a thousand times (a two-proportion z-test). Groups 1 and 2 split this way on four statements, Groups 2 and 3 on six, and Groups 1 and 3 on ten. Groups 1 and 2 split on tying migration numbers to housing and services, and on limits for family visas. Groups 2 and 3 split most on helping refugees and others in need.</p><p data-k="n-chance">Testing many statements can turn up a few differences by luck. So for each pair of groups we also shuffled their members into two random groups of the same sizes, 1,000 times, and ran the same count on each (a permutation test). No shuffle found more than two such statements. Group 2 also agrees with more of the statements than either other group: typically 74% of those it votes on, against 58% in Group 1 and 63% in Group 3.</p><p data-k="n-allies">We also looked for unlikely allies, people in different groups who back the same statements, and found none that held up.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The group cards</h3><p>Group 1’s and Group 3’s cards each show the three statements where most of the group agreed and the other two groups lagged furthest behind. Group 2’s card shows where it sides with each of the other groups, and one it backed more than both. RadicalxChange chose which statements to show. We chose statements about what each group wants, including what it values. Lines that mainly judge other people count in every figure, but aren’t on the cards.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Who and what we counted</h3><p data-k="n-stmts">144 statements were open for voting: the 36 starter statements and 108 written by people taking part. The moderators held back 60 others, so no one else voted on them.</p><p data-k="n-people">We count the 77 people who voted on at least one of those 144 statements. Polis’s own count is 79: it also counts two people whose only votes were on their own statements, which the moderators held back. Between them, the 77 cast 7,106 votes.</p><p data-k="n-own">One person’s only vote was the agreement Polis records when someone adds a statement. They count as taking part, but voted on too few statements to be placed in a group.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Pairing the statements</h3><p>RadicalxChange chose which statements to pair. Every number comes straight from the results.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The priority vote</h3><p data-k="n-qv">A separate vote with its own voters. We set aside ballots that used fewer than 10 credits (there were none). Where someone voted more than once, we counted the last ballot they submitted. That leaves 39 voters. Net votes are votes for minus votes against. The matching statements from the conversation are close in meaning, not identical in wording.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The data</h3><p data-k="n-data">From the Polis export of 20 November 2026, 12:35pm AEDT. The conversation is still open, so these results are preliminary. Statements appear exactly as people wrote them.</p></div>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
